  "semantics": {
    "constraints": "Original English input: one line of 1..100000 plus/minus characters; stdio, no interaction or special checker. With both endpoints fixed, two consecutive crossings with the same upper wire cancel. A stack computes the reduced alternating word in O(n) time/space; uniquely print Yes iff it is empty, otherwise No. Exhaustive deletion searches and an independent oriented-crossing sum cross-check the oracle.",
    "inputLayout": "Exactly one sequence on one line, no separate n/header/test count; optional final LF or CRLF. Characters only ASCII + and -, length 1..100000 inclusive.",
    "output": "One uniquely determined Yes or No token, with exact canonical case. Integer/floating/custom-checker/multiple-valid-output concerns do not apply.",
    "sourceModes": {
      "inputMode": "stdio",
      "interactionFormat": null,
      "generatedChecker": null
    },
    "oracle": "Adjacent crossings of the same upper wire can be removed with endpoints fixed, as explicitly described for ++ and for the middle ++ of -++-. Stack reduction leaves an alternating word; it is untangled iff empty. Runtime O(n), memory O(n).",
    "independentReferences": "Memoized search tries every legal adjacent equal-pair deletion until empty is reachable. This search is structurally different from the left-to-right stack. Separately, each crossing contributes +/-1 with its sign reversed at odd positions because the wires swap sides. Deleting identical consecutive symbols removes opposite contributions and preserves endpoint parity; a nonempty alternating reduced word has nonzero sum. Thus even length and zero oriented sum is an independent exact criterion.",
    "normalization": "No exponent/constraint normalization required: public original HTML states 100000 as plain digits, agreeing with the pinned input_format."
  },
